	Imported apart: `import helix.raylib.extras.Lighting.*;`.

	The example `engines/raylib/examples/extras/lighting` uses it.

	```haxe
	var pbr = LoadPbrShader();
	SetShaderValue(pbr, GetShaderLocation(pbr, "ambient"), [0.1, 0.1, 0.1, 1.0], SHADER_UNIFORM_VEC4);
	for (m in model.materials)
		m.shader = pbr;
	var sun = CreateLight(LIGHT_DIRECTIONAL, new Vector3(4, 10, 2), Vector3Zero(), WHITE, pbr);
	var lamp = CreateLight(LIGHT_POINT, new Vector3(-2, 1, -2), Vector3Zero(), YELLOW, pbr);
	SetLightRange(lamp, 6);
	// each frame
	SetShaderValue(pbr, pbr.locs[SHADER_LOC_VECTOR_VIEW], [camera.position.x, camera.position.y, camera.position.z],
		SHADER_UNIFORM_VEC3);
	```

	**Lights.** rlights' `CreateLight` (`helix.raylib.Rlights`) makes a light of the scene, as many as the program makes. A light shines
	on every lit draw, whichever shader it was made with: `LoadPbrShader`'s, `LoadLightingShader`'s, and a program's own that includes
	`<helix/raylib/shaders/lights.glsl>` or `<helix/raylib/shaders/pbr.glsl>`. Its fields (`enabled`, `position`, `target`, `color`)
	are read at each draw, so a light moves by setting them, with no other call. `UnloadLight` takes it out of the scene.

	**Which lights reach a draw.** Each draw is lit by the 8 lights that reach it most, scored against its bounds: a light's brightness
	(its color times its intensity), times its falloff at the bounds' nearest point (none past its range), times a spot light's cone.
	A directional light reaches everything. A point or spot light without a range reaches everything too, so a scene of many lamps gives
	each one a range. `DrawMeshInstanced` scores its lights against the bounds of all its instances.

	**A program's own shader.** Including `<helix/raylib/shaders/lights.glsl>` gives `lights[MAX_LIGHTS]`, the draw's own lights (8),
	`ambient` and `viewPos`, and `vec4 helixLight(vec4 texelColor, vec4 tint, vec3 fragPosition, vec3 fragNormal)`, called as
	`helixLight(texture(texture0, fragTexCoord), colDiffuse*fragColor, fragPosition, fragNormal)`. Including
	`<helix/raylib/shaders/pbr.glsl>` gives `vec4 helixPbr(vec4 baseColor, float metallic, float roughness, float occlusion, vec3
	emissive, vec3 fragPosition, vec3 normal)`, and the material's `emissiveColor` and `alphaCutoff` (the Materials extra). Both take
	the world position and normal as raylib's `lighting.vs` passes them: `fragPosition` is `vec3(matModel*vec4(vertexPosition, 1.0))`
	and `fragNormal` is `normalize(vec3(matNormal*vec4(vertexNormal, 1.0)))`. A model posed by `UpdateModelAnimation` under a lit shader
	is skinned on the GPU. A program that calls none of this links none of it. */
